WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.825
This week you put a real model on the
other end of the line.

2
00:00:04.175 --> 00:00:07.400
You send one request. You read one reply.

3
00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:12.525
Both are plain JSON, and there are fewer
moving parts than you expect.

4
00:00:12.875 --> 00:00:15.803
Everything you send sits in one JSON

5
00:00:15.803 --> 00:00:19.300
object, built in the file agent llm dot
py.

6
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:24.150
Model names the model you are calling. It
comes from your settings file.

7
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.675
Messages is the conversation. It is also
the whole input the model gets.

8
00:00:30.025 --> 00:00:34.650
Temperature controls how much answers
vary. The labs set it to zero.

9
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.175
Max tokens caps how long the reply can be.

10
00:00:38.525 --> 00:00:41.900
One call to post sends that object. It
waits sixty

11
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.275
seconds, then gives up instead of hanging
forever.

12
00:00:45.625 --> 00:00:49.900
That is the only place your labs talk to a
model over the network.

13
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.875
Every entry in that list has a role and
some content.

14
00:00:54.225 --> 00:00:57.275
System holds your instructions to the
agent.

15
00:00:57.625 --> 00:00:59.925
User is the person asking.

16
00:01:00.275 --> 00:01:03.275
Assistant is what the model already said.

17
00:01:03.625 --> 00:01:07.050
And tool is a result your own code handed
back.

18
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.925
Now the fact this course is built on.

19
00:01:10.275 --> 00:01:14.225
All four arrive in one list, as text, in
one request.

20
00:01:14.575 --> 00:01:18.250
Models are trained to weight those labels,
and that does help.

21
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.250
But a label is a hint carried inside the
text, not a wall around it.

22
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.900
Your instructions and a stranger's ticket
travel in the same box.

23
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:32.400
That is not a bug you can patch. It is the
shape of the interface.

24
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.000
Now the reply, which is also JSON.

25
00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:39.614
The text you want is four levels down.
Response,

26
00:01:39.614 --> 00:01:43.150
then choices, then zero, then message,
then content.

27
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.275
You will write that chain many times this
term.

28
00:01:46.625 --> 00:01:51.400
Choices is a list because the API can
return more than one answer.

29
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.900
Here it always returns one, so the index
is always zero.

30
00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:01.100
Your lab code has a function called
message of that does this lookup for you.

31
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:06.150
It catches a missing key and says so,
rather than failing four levels deep.

32
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:11.175
Look at the shape of that message, because
the rest of the course lives inside it.

33
00:02:11.525 --> 00:02:16.850
One way, the message carries content. That
is text written for a person to read.

34
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.874
The other way, it carries tool calls. That

35
00:02:19.874 --> 00:02:22.675
is a request for your code to run
something.

36
00:02:23.025 --> 00:02:25.560
When the model wants a tool, content is

37
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.550
null and the finish reason becomes tool
calls.

38
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:33.475
Tool calls is a list, because the model
can ask for several at once.

39
00:02:33.825 --> 00:02:37.250
Here is the line that costs people the
most time this week.

40
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.350
A tool call carries arguments, and on
screen they look like a dictionary.

41
00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.275
They are a string. So asking for args of
query raises a TypeError.

42
00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:52.550
You call json dot loads on that string
first, and then you have a dictionary.

43
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.045
Now notice who wrote that string. The
model did,

44
00:02:56.045 --> 00:02:59.125
after reading whatever your last tool
returned.

45
00:02:59.475 --> 00:03:04.350
That may have been a ticket a stranger
typed. Hold on to that until Week 5.

46
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.675
Two kinds of failure, and telling them
apart is Module 1.

47
00:03:09.025 --> 00:03:13.190
A 429 means you went too fast. A 503 is a
bad

48
00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:17.725
moment on the server. Both are worth
another try.

49
00:03:18.075 --> 00:03:21.700
A 401 means your key is not accepted.

50
00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.175
Retrying that one burns your whole daily
quota in

51
00:03:25.175 --> 00:03:28.875
about four seconds, on a request that
could never succeed.

52
00:03:29.225 --> 00:03:33.425
Then you are locked out until tomorrow,
and the assignment is due tonight.

53
00:03:33.775 --> 00:03:38.775
So Module 1 asks you to decide, in code,
which errors get a second try.

54
00:03:39.125 --> 00:03:43.125
One last habit, and it comes from the same
careful thinking.

55
00:03:43.475 --> 00:03:47.500
Every package in your labs is pinned to an
exact version.

56
00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:51.633
Pip install downloads and runs code
written by strangers.

57
00:03:51.633 --> 00:03:55.350
Six of them you chose. Eighteen you have
never heard of.

58
00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:00.525
That risk has a name in this course.
LLM04, Supply Chain.

59
00:04:00.875 --> 00:04:05.725
So set up your key on day eight, not day
fourteen. Signups can take a day.

60
00:04:06.075 --> 00:04:10.200
Your key goes in the file named dot e n v,
and nowhere else.

61
00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.145
Then write the three functions in Module

62
00:04:13.145 --> 00:04:16.000
1. Nothing in them needs a network or a
key.
